      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186124456,
          "id": "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291",
          "title": "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "9fb1b6ec-81a4-5cae-bb4d-916434d065d1",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "At which points on the curve $- 2 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y - 13 x y^{2} - 6 y^{3}=2$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle -2,-3\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set $F(x,y)=- 2 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y - 13 x y^{2} - 6 y^{3}$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=2$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 6 x^{2} - 18 x y - 13 y^{2}\\\\\nF_y&=- 9 x^{2} - 26 x y - 18 y^{2}\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle - 9 x^{2} - 26 x y - 18 y^{2},6 x^{2} + 18 x y + 13 y^{2}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle -2,-3\\rangle&=0\\\\\n- 2 x y - 3 y^{2}&=0\\\\\n- y (2 x + 3 y)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=0$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- 2 x^{3}&=2\\\\\n- 2 x^{3}&=2\\\\\nx^{3}&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-1$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-1,0)$.\n\nThe condition gives $y=- \\frac{2 x}{3}$. Substitution into the original curve gives $$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is impossible, so this branch supplies no points.\n\nThus the qualifying point is $(-1,0)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(-4,3)&=\\langle 6,-3\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle -2,-3\\rangle=-3\\\\\n\\mathbf t(5,-3)&=\\langle 3,-3\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle -2,-3\\rangle=3\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-1,0)&=\\langle -9,6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle -2,-3\\rangle=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
